## How to Apply the Schema

### Upgrading an existing database (IMPORTANT)

`create table if not exists` will **never update tables that already exist**. If you
ran an older version of this schema (or the legacy per-table files), your tables use
single-column primary keys (`pat_num`), have no `clinic_id` column, and no
`subscription_tier` / `max_patients` fields. Re-running the consolidated file
"passes" but leaves those tables unchanged, because PostgreSQL only checks the table
**name** — it ignores any added/changed columns, composite keys, or foreign keys.

To apply the new structure you must drop the old tables first, then re-create them:

### Option 1: Supabase SQL Editor (reset + recreate)
1. Open Supabase Dashboard → SQL Editor
2. Paste the contents of `supabase-reset-schema.sql`  →  Click "Run" (drops the old tables/views)
3. Paste the contents of `supabase-multitenant-schema.sql` → Click "Run" (creates the new structure + seed)

> ⚠️ **Reset is destructive** — only run it on a dev/test database or one you can re-seed.
> It drops every table managed by the multi-tenant schema. Rerunning the consolidated
> SQL restores the three sample clinics (A/B/C).

### Option 2: Supabase CLI
```bash
supabase db push --file supabase-multitenant-schema.sql
```

### Option 3: psql
```bash
psql "postgresql://user:password@db.supabase.co:5432/postgres" \
  -f supabase-multitenant-schema.sql
```

The script is **idempotent** — safe to run multiple times (uses `create table if not exists`, `drop policy if exists`, `create or replace`).

